
  <sn><md>### Histosols {.text-center}

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-md-9
**Concept:**

- Histosols (from the Greek *histos* – `tissue`) are mainly `organic rich soils` under permanent water-saturated conditions with a thick, peaty horizon termed as `histic epipedon`. 
- The Histosol order contains soils commonly called `bogs, moors, peat lands, muskegs, fens and mucks`. 
- They have very `low bulk densities` ($&lt;0.3\text{ g/cm}^3$) and very `high water holding capacity`. Histosols make up about `1% of the world's ice-free land surface`.
